  "full_response": "The **Actor Model** is a conceptual framework for concurrent and distributed computing, first proposed by Carl Hewitt in 1973 and later developed by Gul Agha. It provides a high-level abstraction for writing concurrent programs by treating \"actors\" as the fundamental units of computation. Here’s a detailed breakdown:\n\n---\n\n### **Core Principles of the Actor Model**\n1. **Actors as Universal Primitives**\n   - Everything is an **actor**: a lightweight, isolated computational entity that encapsulates state, behavior, and communication.\n   - Actors are analogous to \"objects\" in object-oriented programming but with key differences (e.g., no shared state, asynchronous messaging).\n\n2. **Message Passing**\n   - Actors communicate **only** via **asynchronous messages** (no shared memory or direct method calls).\n   - Messages are sent to an actor’s **mailbox** (a FIFO queue) and processed one at a time.\n   - This avoids race conditions and the need for locks/mutexes.\n\n3. **Isolation and Encapsulation**\n   - An actor’s **internal state** is private and can only be modified by the actor itself in response to messages.\n   - No direct access to another actor’s state (unlike objects in OOP).\n\n4. **No Shared State**\n   - Actors do not share memory; they interact solely through messages.\n   - This eliminates issues like deadlocks, livelocks, or data races common in thread-based concurrency.\n\n5. **Behavior-Driven Execution**\n   - An actor’s behavior defines how it processes messages.\n   - Upon receiving a message, an actor can:\n     - **Send messages** to other actors.\n     - **Create new actors**.\n     - **Designate a new behavior** for the next message (e.g., change its state).\n\n6. **Location Transparency**\n   - Actors can reside on the same machine or be distributed across a network without changing the programming model.\n   - The model abstracts away physical location (useful for distributed systems).\n\n---\n\n### **How Actors Work**\n1. **Mailbox**\n   - Each actor has a mailbox where incoming messages are queued.\n   - Messages are processed **sequentially** (no parallelism within an actor).\n\n2. **Message Processing**\n   - When an actor processes a message, it:\n     - **Reads** the message from its mailbox.\n     - **Executes** its current behavior (logic) to handle the message.\n    ",
